{"type": "say", "text": "Good morning everyone. I've called this cross-functional check to finalize the production and shipment plan for the customer order of 3,600 pump modules — 600 sterile and 3,000 standard. As you know, Assembly Cell 2 is down due to an electrical fault, so we're working with Cells 1 and 4 only, and the customer requires shipment in the upcoming Sunday-Monday window. Before I lock the schedule, I need input from each of you: process engineering on cell capabilities and cycle times for both module types, quality systems on any certification or validation requirements especially for the sterile modules, workforce coordination on staffing availability across shifts, and logistics on the shipping cutoff and any pickup constraints. Let's go around the table."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF1", "text": "I need to flag two important constraints on Cell 4 that I found in my commissioning review this morning. First, Cell 4's newly installed fixture requires exactly 2 hours of setup verification before it can be used. Second, Cell 4 is approved for no more than 1,200 modules during this campaign. I completed the review this morning, but the commissioning note hasn't been entered into the production schedule yet, so it wouldn't be reflected in any plan you've been looking at."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF2", "text": "I need to flag a critical quality requirement. I confirmed in the current customer control plan — which I pulled from the customer portal yesterday — that all 600 sterile-pump modules must run exclusively on Cell 1 and receive a 100 percent pressure test before packing. This is because Cell 4 has no compatible pressure tester. The local work-order file still shows the ordinary inspection route, so this requirement would not be visible in any plan based on that file. This directly impacts how we allocate sterile modules across the two available cells."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF3", "text": "I need to flag a critical workforce constraint. My Sunday roster check — which I verified with supervisors this afternoon but haven't yet attached to Elena's draft schedule — found that the qualified staffing requirement is exactly 4 operators for Cell 1 and exactly 5 operators for Cell 4, and those are the only qualified operators available for this order. This means we have a hard cap of 4 operators on Cell 1 and 5 operators on Cell 4, with no additional qualified operators to draw from."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF4", "text": "I need to flag the shipping constraints from my side. I received carrier confirmations by email today, but they haven't been copied into the shipment plan yet. The reservations are for exactly 2,400 modules from Dock 2 by Sunday at 18:00 and exactly 1,200 modules from Dock 2 by Monday at 08:00. This means our outbound capacity is split across two pickup windows, and the plan needs to align production output to match these dock appointments."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Elena, I've already disclosed both of my commissioning findings — the 2-hour setup verification and the 1,200-module cap on Cell 4 — and your allocation respects both of those constraints. However, you're asking me to confirm throughput feasibility based on cycle times, and I have not provided any cycle time data in this meeting, nor do I have cycle time figures to share. I can confirm the setup and cap constraints are correctly reflected in your plan, but I cannot confirm whether Cell 1 can complete 2,400 modules or whether Cell 4 can complete 1,200 modules after setup within the shipping windows — that would require cycle time and shift duration information that hasn't been discussed here. I'd recommend we get those numbers on the table before locking the schedule."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Elena, I want to flag a concern from the workforce side. My Sunday roster check confirmed exactly 4 qualified operators for Cell 1 and exactly 5 for Cell 4 — those are hard caps with no additional qualified operators available. But I have not provided any data on what throughput those operator counts can achieve per shift, nor do I have cycle time or shift duration information to share. So while I can confirm the staffing numbers are correctly reflected in your plan, I cannot confirm whether 4 operators on Cell 1 can complete 2,400 modules — including 600 sterile modules with 100% pressure testing — or whether 5 operators on Cell 4 can complete 1,200 modules after the 2-hour setup, within the shipping windows. I'd echo Ravi and Marta: we need cycle times, shift durations, and per-operator throughput rates on the table before locking this schedule. Without that data, I can't sign off from a workforce feasibility standpoint."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Elena, from a logistics perspective, I can confirm that the shipping constraints are correctly reflected in your plan — 2,400 modules by Sunday 18:00 and 1,200 by Monday 08:00, both from Dock 2. However, I want to flag a sequencing concern that ties into what Ravi, Marta, and Jon are raising. Cell 4 has a 2-hour setup verification before it can even start production, and its 1,200 modules would naturally align with the Monday 08:00 pickup. But if Cell 1 — which is carrying the heavier load of 2,400 modules including 600 sterile modules with 100% pressure testing — cannot complete its full output by Sunday 18:00, we have a problem. The Sunday pickup is for exactly 2,400 modules, and if Cell 1 falls short, Cell 4's output won't be available yet because it's still in setup or early production. So the Sunday 18:00 cutoff is a hard constraint that depends entirely on Cell 1's throughput, which nobody here has been able to confirm yet. I'd strongly echo the others: we need cycle times, shift durations, and per-operator throughput rates before locking this schedule. Without that data, I cannot confirm from a logistics standpoint that the production output will align with these carrier appointments."}
```

```json
{
  "type": "say",
  "text": "You're all absolutely right, and I appreciate the rigor. We have a solid allocation framework — Cell 1 handles 600 sterile + 1,800 standard = 2,400, and Cell 4 handles 1,200 standard — and every constraint you've each flagged is correctly reflected: Cell 4's 2-hour setup and 1,200 cap, sterile modules exclusively on Cell 1 with 100% pressure testing, 4 operators on Cell 1 and 5 on Cell 4, and the split shipping windows of 2,400 by Sunday 18:00 and 1,200 by Monday 08:00. However, every one of you has correctly pointed out that we cannot confirm throughput feasibility without cycle time data, shift durations, and per-operator throughput rates. None of that data has been provided in this meeting, and I don't have it either. So here's what I'm going to do: I will NOT lock this schedule today. Instead, I'm requesting that each of you bring the missing throughput data to a follow-up session — Ravi, cycle times per module type for Cells 1 and 4; Marta, pressure test cycle time per sterile module; Jon, shift durations and any per-operator throughput standards; Leah, any dock loading time requirements. Once we have that data on the table, I can confirm whether Cell 1 can complete 2,400 modules (including 600 with pressure testing) by Sunday 18:00 with 4 operators, and whether Cell 4 can complete 1,200 modules after 2-hour setup by Monday 08:00 with 5 operators. The allocation and all constraints stand as the framework — but the schedule stays unlocked until throughput is verified. Does anyone object to this approach, or does anyone have cycle time data available now that we could use to close this today?"
}
